=======================================================================
FIRST-PARTY — sha512mb
=======================================================================

sha512mb, including the AVX2 and AVX-512 multi-buffer kernels in
sha512mb/native_amd64.s, is original Overclock Validator work implementing
the SHA-512 compression function of NIST FIPS 180-4, using that standard's
published initial hash value and round constants. It is not derived from
Firedancer, OpenSSL, or any other implementation.


=======================================================================
DESIGN INFLUENCES — internal/r51x5
=======================================================================

The radix-2^51 five-limb, lane-per-signature layout is Narya's own
implementation. It was motivated by independent published evidence that the
representation suits AVX-512 IFMA:

  * ENG25519, Zhang et al., USENIX Security 2024
    https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity24/presentation/zhang-jipeng
  * "Breaking 10 million TPS", Eclipse Labs
    https://www.eclipselabs.io/blogs/breaking-10-million-tps

Neither source establishes Narya's acceptance predicate or its performance,
and no code was taken from either.


=======================================================================
RESEARCH TRACK — internal/heea8l
=======================================================================

The half-extended-Euclidean scalar decomposition track follows the artifact
and paper archived at https://artifacts.iacr.org/tches/2025/a26/
(IACR Transactions on Cryptographic Hardware and Embedded Systems, 2025).
The comparison baseline quoted in docs/HEEA.md is that artifact's, measured
against ed25519-donna (Andrew Moon). This code is a research prototype and
is not part of any production build.


=======================================================================
SPECIFICATIONS AND REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATIONS
=======================================================================

Narya implements behavior specified elsewhere. No code is taken from these
sources; they are recorded because the behavior they define is the product.

  * ed25519-dalek (dalek-cryptography) — the DalekStrict profile reproduces
    the acceptance predicate of its verify_strict, and
    internal/edwards25519/smallorder.go mirrors EdwardsPoint::is_small_order.
    https://github.com/dalek-cryptography/curve25519-dalek
  * Agave (Anza) — the Solana validator whose verdict Narya must match byte
    for byte; it reaches verify_strict through the solana-signature crate.
    https://github.com/anza-xyz/agave
  * Solana Foundation — SIMD-0376 (proposed verify_strict loosening) and
    SIMD-0152, referenced in docs/REVIEW-NOTES.md.
  * Zcash ZIP 215 — the reserved ZIP215 profile name.
    https://zips.z.cash/zip-0215
  * BoringSSL — the square-root-ratio derivation used in
    internal/edwards25519/field/fe.go and internal/r43x6/arithmetic.go.
    https://boringssl.googlesource.com/boringssl/
  * NIST FIPS 180-4 — SHA-512, implemented by sha512mb.


=======================================================================
COMPARISON AND DIFFERENTIAL ORACLE
=======================================================================

curve25519-voi (Oasis Protocol Foundation, BSD-3-Clause,
https://github.com/oasisprotocol/curve25519-voi) is used as a
cross-library differential oracle for the acceptance predicate and as a
performance baseline. It is a test-only dependency behind the
oasis_compare build tag and a separate module file; no curve25519-voi code
is redistributed with Narya. Narya does not implement its ABGLSV-Pornin
cofactored algorithm, which is incompatible with the strict predicate.

curve25519-voi is itself, by its own account, "mostly derived from
curve25519-dalek" (https://github.com/dalek-cryptography/curve25519-dalek).
That lineage is worth recording explicitly here, because the specific part
of voi that Narya measures against is not voi's own work:

    curve/edwards_vector_amd64.{go,s}
    Copyright (c) 2016-2019 isis agora lovecruft
    Copyright (c) 2016-2019 Henry de Valence
    Copyright (c) 2021 Oasis Labs Inc.
    BSD-3-Clause

is a Go port of curve25519-dalek's AVX2 vectorized Edwards backend, using
that backend's radix-25.5 four-way field representation. It is selected
whenever cpu.X86.HasAVX2 holds, so voi's uncached single-signature timings
are vectorized results rather than scalar ones.

Narya's own coordinate-parallel packed-x4 work reaches the same
intra-signature orientation independently, from Firedancer's r43x6 rather
than from dalek, and at radix 2^51 on AVX-512 IFMA rather than radix 25.5
on AVX2. No dalek or voi code was consulted or copied in its development.
The parallel is recorded because a reader comparing the two benchmark
columns should know they are comparing the same architectural idea, and
because credit for that idea belongs to the curve25519-dalek authors.


